## EXP-003 in plain English

**What it did:** We built a flight simulator for the trading strategy and flew five versions of it through all of 2024, minute by minute, with realistic trading costs (fees, slippage, funding). The only difference between the five versions was *who decides when to buy* — everything else (data, costs, risk limits) was identical. Starting money: $10,000 each.

**The five pilots:**
- **A — Coin-flip trader.** Buys/sells randomly. The "are we better than dumb luck?" baseline.
- **B — Quant only.** Buys when our LightGBM model says there's ≥40% chance of a profitable 15-minute move.
- **C — Quant + policy rules.** Same as B, but the decision goes through our policy checklist. (Turned out identical to B — the checklist added nothing.)
- **D — Quant + mock-Jev + policy.** The full stack: model + Jev's opinion + checklist.
- **E — Policy with Jev muted.** Never traded once — proving the policy does nothing without Jev input.

**Results (all of 2024):**

| Pilot | Trades | Win rate | Profit on $10k |
|---|---|---|---|
| A Random | 10,221 | 21% | **−$1,435** |
| B Quant | 4,897 | 49% | **−$626** |
| C Quant+policy | 4,897 | 49% | **−$626** |
| D Full stack | 656 | 48% | **−$84** |
| E Muted | 0 | — | $0 |

**What it means, in one paragraph:** The model *can* rank trades — when we only took its most confident calls, the win rate rose from 49% to 56%. But each trade earns roughly **−1 cent before costs and costs 10 cents in fees**. So even the best version bleeds slowly, and cranking fees up 2–3× (the "hostile" test) just bleeds faster. The full stack's smaller loss (−$84 vs −$626) looks nice until you see why: Jev just vetoed 87% of trades without picking winners any better (48% vs 49% wins). It lost less by *doing less*, not by *knowing more*.

**The verdict:** the strategy's edge is real but tiny — smaller than the price of trading itself. Like having a car that's slightly more fuel-efficient than average but you still lose money on every trip because of tolls. That's why the plan says stop and rethink rather than go live: no version survived costs, so there's nothing to deploy yet.

